This page is the first page of a factor table: for a = 1 to 58 it lists the prime factorizations of a²+1 ('Diuisores ipsius aa+1'). The table continues on the next page and later beyond 100.

It serves the paper's programme of producing large primes from quadratic forms. Every odd prime divisor of a²+1 is ≡ 1 (mod 4), since −1 must be a quadratic residue. Also, p divides a²+1 exactly when a ≡ ±r (mod p), where r² ≡ −1 (mod p). The table therefore exhibits the periodic pattern of divisors that Euler uses as a sieve:
- 5 divides a²+1 iff a ≡ 2, 3 (mod 5);
- 13 divides it iff a ≡ 5, 8 (mod 13);
- 17 divides it iff a ≡ 4, 13 (mod 17);
- and so on.

Whenever no such p ≤ √(a²+1) divides it, a²+1 (or (a²+1)/2, (a²+1)/5, (a²+1)/10, the forms treated on the previous page) is prime. The values of a with a²+1 prime here are 1, 2, 4, 6, 10, 14, 16, 20, 24, 26, 36, 40, 54, 56.
